<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">South Seas Mandate</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1947 CE · capital Koror</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Micronesian islands Japan took from Germany in 1914 and governed under a League of Nations mandate, later fortified and fought over in the Pacific War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Japanese navy seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> island colonies north of the equator: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a> except <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/guam">Guam</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Carolines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshalls</a>. In 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> gave them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as a mandate, and in 1922 a civilian South Seas Government was set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koror">Koror</a> in Palau.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinian">Tinian</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-seas-development-company">South Seas Development Company</a>, phosphate mining and fisheries. Japanese settlers, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa</a>, came to outnumber the islanders by the late 1930s, and schools taught Japanese. Japan left the League in 1933 but kept the islands and turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuuk-lagoon">Truk</a> into a major naval base.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>American forces captured the islands in 1944 and 1945 in battles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peleliu">Peleliu</a> and elsewhere; the atomic bomb missions flew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinian">Tinian</a>. In 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> placed them under American trusteeship, from which today's island states emerged.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/south-seas-mandate.jpg" alt="South Seas Mandate" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: kahusi - (Talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan_(1870%E2%80%931999).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1947 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Koror</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese, Chamorro, Palauan, Marshallese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/palau">Palau</a>, Federated States of Micronesia, <a href="/countries/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a>, <a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nan'yo, South Pacific Mandate, Nanyo Cho, Nanyo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1933&amp;focus=south-seas-mandate" aria-label="South Seas Mandate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8401 1903 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where South Seas Mandate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8721 2159L9094 2680M8721 2159L8529 1145M8721 2159L9188 2042"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9094 2680h0M8529 1145h0M9188 2042h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8721 2159h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8721 2159h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Koror. <a href="/atlas?year=1933&amp;focus=south-seas-mandate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
